# Asserted test-count floors — ONE definition, sourced by every gate.
#
# These scripts assert the COUNT, not the exit code, because a runner whose glob
# matches nothing exits 0 having run zero tests. That design only works if the
# numbers are right, and they had drifted: the same Kotlin floor was written in
# two places (one as a hand-summed total), and the appd floor was >0 in the script
# that puts the DAEMON into production while the desktop release demanded >=300 —
# so the production path accepted a suite that had lost 90% of its tests.
#
# Raise a floor here when tests are added; never edit a copy in a script.

KOTLIN_SHARED_MIN=838    # :core jvm + :core android + :app + :ui jvm   (actual 842 on 2026-08-25)
DESKTOP_EXTRA_MIN=170    # + :app-desktop, added on top of the shared floor (actual 177 on 2026-08-23)
#
# Was 189 from the day these floors were consolidated, which was ALWAYS above the
# real count — this file's own header says the numbers had drifted and one was a
# hand-summed total, and 189 was the hand-summed one. Checked 2026-08-23: no
# :app-desktop test file has been deleted since, and 14 remain, so nothing was
# lost; the number was simply never true. It never fired because the assertion is
# on the SUM (KOTLIN_SHARED_MIN + DESKTOP_EXTRA_MIN), which the shared half kept
# clearing on its own — a floor that cannot fail is not a floor.
APPD_MIN=630             # server/appd node --test  (actual 634 on 2026-08-25)
